@page "/dustbin/iframe"

<PageTitle>Dustbin - Within an iframe · Bdnd</PageTitle>

<div class="bb-intro">
    <h1>Dustbin - Within an iframe</h1>
    <p class="bb-subtitle">
        The HTML5 backend works inside an iframe because Bdnd listens to the native drag
        events of whatever document it runs in. Below, a self-contained dustbin demo is hosted
        in its own iframe document - dragging works entirely within it.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="bb-demo">
    <div class="bb-demo-bar"><span class="bb-demo-label">iframe · dustbin/single</span></div>

    @* The iframe is rendered only once this page runs in the browser. A prerendered one is replaced, together with
       the rest of the prerendered markup, when WebAssembly takes the page over: the page inside it would be loaded
       twice, its first boot aborted halfway. data-bdnd-frame tells the framed page to show its sample and none of
       the site around it (App.razor). *@
    @if (RendererInfo.IsInteractive)
    {
        <iframe src="dustbin/single" title="Dustbin inside an iframe" class="demo-iframe" data-bdnd-frame></iframe>
    }
    else
    {
        <div class="demo-iframe"></div>
    }
</div>

<DemoCode />

<div class="bb-note">
    A single <code>BdndProvider</code> (and its <code>BdndDragDropManager</code>) lives in one
    document, so dragging <em>across</em> the iframe boundary into the parent page is not
    supported by the HTML5 backend - the same limitation React DnD has without a
    cross-window backend setup.
</div>
